      "text": "To determine the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we first express each term in the sequence as follows:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\text{ for } n = 1, 2, \\ldots, 1000.\n\\]\nWe need to find the distinct values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000. Let's analyze the range of \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) as \\(n\\) varies from 1 to 1000:\n\\[\n\\frac{1^2}{1000} = \\frac{1}{1000} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{1000^2}{1000} = 1000.\n\\]\nThus, \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) ranges from \\(\\frac{1}{1000}\\) to 1000. When \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) is an integer, \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) is that integer. We need to determine the number of distinct integers \\(k\\) such that:\n\\[\nk = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\text{ for some integer } n \\text{ from 1 to 1000}.\n\\]\nThe smallest value of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) is 0 and the largest value is 999. We need to find the number of distinct integers \\(k\\) in the range from 0 to 999. These integers are the possible values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000. To confirm the number of such integers, we note that \\(k = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) implies:\n\\[\nk \\leq \\frac{n^2}{1000} < k+1.\n\\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n1000k \\leq n^2 < 1000(k+1).\n\\]\nSince \\(n\\) ranges from 1 to 1000, we have:\n\\[\n1000k \\leq n^2 < 1000k + 1000.\n\\]\nFor each \\(k\\), \\(k\\) is valid for some \\(n\\) because \\(1000k\\) can be the square of at least \\(10\\sqrt{k}\\) and \\(1000k + 1000\\) can be just greater than the square of \\(1000\\), but \\(n^2\\) can never reach this value as \\(n\\) grows larger than 1000. Therefore, \\(k\\) can take on any integer value from 0 to 999, inclusive. This gives us 1000 distinct values.\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1000}.\n\\]",
