      "text": "To determine the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we need to analyze the expression \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 1000.\n\nFirst, let's find the range of values that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) can take. We need to determine the smallest and largest values of \\(k\\) such that \\(k \\leq \\frac{n^2}{1000} < k+1\\). This inequality can be rewritten as:\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{n^2}{1000} < k+1 \\]\nMultiplying through by 1000 gives:\n\\[ 1000k \\leq n^2 < 1000(k+1) \\]\nTaking the square root of all parts, we get:\n\\[ \\sqrt{1000k} \\leq n < \\sqrt{1000(k+1)} \\]\nWe want to find the smallest \\(k\\) such that \\(n^2 \\geq 1000k\\). For the smallest \\(k\\), we set \\(n = 1\\):\n\\[ 1000k \\leq 1^2 = 1 \\]\nThis is not possible, so we take the next integer, \\(k = 1\\):\n\\[ 1000 \\cdot 1 \\leq n^2 < 1000 \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ 1000 \\leq n^2 < 2000 \\]\nThe largest integer \\(n\\) that satisfies this inequality is \\(n = 44\\) because \\(44^2 = 1936\\) and \\(45^2 = 2025\\), which is just above 2000. Therefore, \\(k = 1\\) is valid for \\(n = 1\\) to \\(44\\), and we need to check if there are any other possible values of \\(k\\).\n\nFor \\(k = 2\\):\n\\[ 1000 \\cdot 2 \\leq n^2 < 1000 \\cdot 3 \\]\n\\[ 2000 \\leq n^2 < 3000 \\]\nThe largest integer \\(n\\) that satisfies this inequality is \\(n = 45\\) because \\(45^2 = 2025\\) and \\(46^2 = 2116\\), which is just above 2000. Therefore, \\(k = 2\\) is valid for \\(n = 45\\) to \\(1000\\).\n\nWe can see that the values of \\(k\\) will form a sequence from 1 to 44, and we need to check if there is any overlap in the values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\). The critical point is when \\(k = 44\\), and \\(k = 45\\). For \\(k = 45\\), the range of \\(n\\) is from 45 to 1000, and the value of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) will be the same for \\(n\\) in this range as it was for \\(k = 44\\) and \\(k = 45\\) in the previous range.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) is the number of different values of \\(k\\) from 1 to 44, which is 44.\n\nThe number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{44}\\).",
